  "steelman": "Consolidating healthcare financing into a single universal public system could eliminate private insurance overhead, billing complexity, and insurer profits, while granting the federal government unified bargaining power to negotiate lower prices for pharmaceuticals and medical services.",
  "finding": "Economic analyses of single-payer systems project a range of potential financial outcomes depending on policy choices such as provider reimbursement rates, administrative efficiencies, and coverage generosity. In a systematic review published in PLOS Medicine, researchers found that 19 (86%) of the analyses predicted net savings (with a median net result of a savings of 3.46% of total costs) in the first year of program operation, and 20 (91%) predicted savings over several years. However, modeling by the Congressional Budget Office projected that national health expenditures in 2030 would change by amounts ranging from a decrease of $0.7 trillion to an increase of $0.3 trillion, indicating that overall national spending could decrease or increase depending on how the system is designed.",
  "reasoning_flags": [
    "Forecast, not a finding"
  ],
  "newest_data": "The analysis relies on the Congressional Budget Office's single-payer cost modeling report (Working Paper 2020-08, published December 10, 2020; reaffirmed in subsequent testimony) and the systematic review of single-payer economic models by Cai et al. in PLOS Medicine (published January 15, 2020). A check of the CBO publications database and peer-reviewed literature through October 2026 confirmed no newer comprehensive federal cost simulation or systematic review of nationwide single-payer proposals has superseded these benchmark studies.",
